 doctor to  the point at which we next see him and  what had to happen in between that's all  that's a big dot to leave to the  audience to connect on their own I'm not  sure it totally works um at least will  you stop and think about it because  again Dave is so it does work in the  sense of it's such a yeah a moment that  you don't really necessarily stop and  think about it unless you're doing it on  a rewatch like I do although I do  remember noticing that before we get  Torchwood which is kind of interesting  how it's played out here and the the  various people within it it's very you  know it feels like a very we're just  here to do the job plus you know the one  person at the top who's very into the  patriotism of it all we get we get a bit  of Jackie and Pete you know I don't  remember ever being kind of choked up on  a little bit touched about the two of  them meaning for the first while this  version of Jackie and this version of  Pete meeting for the first time but now  that worked surprisingly well there was  something about their the conflict that  each of them had about knowing this  wasn't truly the same person who they  loved and lost but it is so close that  they can't help but get swept up in it I  that was surprisingly touching so that  was nice Mickey back awesome and there's  just like like I said there's there's a  lot of little things that are just a  little bit off the the whole thing with  the ghosts and people you know except me  and Jackie even being like why do you  have to spoil it why can't it be real I  don't know if Davies is like trying to  make a jab at at faith not specifically  like you know a specific religion but  like faith in general the idea of you  know wanting to believe so hard it feels  like he thought he had a point there but  it's just kind of there  isn't really developed into much of  anything there's well there's the  there's the offhanded dig on Harriet  Jones but you don't care for cuz I think  Harry Jones got the 