 each other  for a few minutes before try and blow  each other off with the more emotional  gut punches of the tail-end so I think  for as much as I I will always say this  series is very up-and-down and very  uneven and very wonky that specific  thread running through that thematic  thing as opposed to you know the mystery  box of ooh Torchwood Torchwood ooh how's  that gonna be which I don't like and I  never have but that sort of thematic  linking and and payoff in the finale  that I actually quite admire and I like  that quite a bit I'm not sure I've got  too much else to say about it it's it's  just a good time with a strong emotional  conclusion and a lot of like little  bumps in the middle that in your at  least for me and my memory they smooth  out basically within minutes of me  finishing having watched an episode of  it or having you know finished watching  this story like yeah there were pause  but like I can't remember them now they  don't seem that important and what  you're left with is the sense of fun and  the sadness and that's what ultimately  should linger so um and while I would  never claim that this is the best series  finale I do think it's a very successful  one especially for what it was trying to  do and at least speaking personally for  the what it leaves behind because what  it leaves behind is the feelings and the  emotions and not the nitpicks at least  not for me so that wraps up series to  doomsday and the army of ghosts this  two-parter oh oh god I'll just mention  this real quick cuz this is like a weird  thing for me we also have Freema Agyeman  you know here not playing Martha later  revealed to be Martha's cousin what  with movies and TV shows the concept of  identical cousins are like identical you  know relatives but who aren't twins  that's like that's such a movie and TV  thing and it's it's ridiculous and I  know like she I pretty sure she hadn't  been cast as as Martha yet at the time  that that she was cast into this but  